WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:02.585
Advances in technology
are always met

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:05.714
with some degree of
technophobia and villainization.

00:00:05.797 --> 00:00:07.048
From the very beginning,

00:00:07.132 --> 00:00:09.676
mechanization has been
perceived as a promise

00:00:09.759 --> 00:00:10.969
and a threat.

00:00:11.052 --> 00:00:12.971
Talk of humans being
replaced by machines

00:00:13.054 --> 00:00:14.556
immediately emerged

00:00:14.639 --> 00:00:17.934
alongside voices praising
their life-enhancing benefits.

00:00:18.017 --> 00:00:20.854
Cyborgs are a unique
amalgamation of both ideas

00:00:20.937 --> 00:00:24.983
since they directly
merge flesh with tech.

00:00:25.066 --> 00:00:28.445
Some of pop culture's most
iconic villains are cyborgs,

00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:30.613
but so are some of the heroes.

00:00:30.697 --> 00:00:32.741
What is it about the
blending of man and machine

00:00:32.824 --> 00:00:35.660
that scares us but
also thrills us?

00:00:35.744 --> 00:00:36.828
[exciting orchestral music]

00:00:36.911 --> 00:00:40.457
I'm Dr. Emily Zarka,
and this is Monstrum.

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:42.250
The impact of industrialization

00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:45.628
and mechanization are crucial
to the cyborg's history,

00:00:45.712 --> 00:00:47.630
but that's not where
the idea began.

00:00:47.714 --> 00:00:49.424
We have to go way back.

00:00:49.507 --> 00:00:51.551
Stories of human
automatons date back

00:00:51.634 --> 00:00:54.596
as far as the fourth
or fifth century BCE.

00:00:54.679 --> 00:00:56.598
The Chinese philosophical
texts "Liezi"

00:00:56.681 --> 00:00:58.266
includes mention of a play

00:00:58.349 --> 00:01:01.269
that utilized a mechanical
puppet made from wood

00:01:01.352 --> 00:01:04.022
and leather that could
independently move its head,

00:01:04.105 --> 00:01:05.607
sing, and dance.

00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:07.901
Records of steam-powered
human-like automatons

00:01:07.984 --> 00:01:11.112
appear in the text of
Alexandrian engineers in Egypt

00:01:11.196 --> 00:01:12.947
around the Third Century CE.

00:01:13.031 --> 00:01:14.908
There are also tales
of automated soldiers

00:01:14.991 --> 00:01:18.203
protecting relics in
ancient Indian Buddhist texts.

00:01:18.286 --> 00:01:19.287
In both fiction and real life,

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:21.664
automatons were
intriguing rarities

00:01:21.748 --> 00:01:23.500
prized for their unusualness

00:01:23.583 --> 00:01:25.710
and ability to inspire wonder.

00:01:25.794 --> 00:01:29.714
And yes, obviously cyborgs
are different from automatons,

00:01:29.798 --> 00:01:32.675
but the two have a
blended cultural history.

00:01:32.759 --> 00:01:36.137
A cyborg is a living
organism machine hybrid

00:01:36.221 --> 00:01:38.640
while an automaton is
a machine made to look

00:01:38.723 --> 00:01:40.391
and or act human.

00:01:40.475 --> 00:01:42.018
Legends of brazen heads,

00:01:42.101 --> 00:01:45.104
bronze or brass talking
fortune telling automations

00:01:45.188 --> 00:01:47.440
modeled after humans
fill the pages

00:01:47.524 --> 00:01:50.652
and stages of the
Renaissance period.

00:01:50.735 --> 00:01:52.612
First appearing in
the 12th century text

00:01:52.695 --> 00:01:54.614
"Chronicle of the
Kings of England,"

00:01:54.697 --> 00:01:56.783
they were often said to
be powered by the devil,

00:01:56.866 --> 00:01:59.035
sorcery, or necromancy.

00:01:59.118 --> 00:02:01.996
So these disembodied heads
were viewed as monstrous,

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:03.581
defying natural order

00:02:03.665 --> 00:02:05.500
and challenging the
idea of the soul

00:02:05.583 --> 00:02:08.795
by raising questions about
what moved our own bodies,

00:02:08.878 --> 00:02:10.755
what imbued life in a thing.

00:02:10.839 --> 00:02:12.048
In the 17th century,

00:02:12.131 --> 00:02:14.717
French philosopher and
mathematician Rene Descartes

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:17.846
introduced the
beast-machine hypothesis,

00:02:17.929 --> 00:02:19.931
theorizing that the
human body is composed

00:02:20.014 --> 00:02:22.767
of mechanical
processes or motions.

00:02:22.851 --> 00:02:25.728
As medicine emerged as
a profession around this time,

00:02:25.812 --> 00:02:29.190
so did the understanding of
the body as an organic machine

00:02:29.274 --> 00:02:31.317
supporting Descartes' theory.

00:02:31.401 --> 00:02:34.112
Of course, this overlaps with
the Industrial Revolution

00:02:34.195 --> 00:02:36.948
and political revolutions
across the world.

00:02:37.031 --> 00:02:38.575
In science and culture,

00:02:38.658 --> 00:02:40.910
the concept of the body
was becoming destabilized

00:02:40.994 --> 00:02:42.495
at the same time

00:02:42.579 --> 00:02:44.998
the manufacturing process
shifted from human hands

00:02:45.081 --> 00:02:46.833
to mechanized devices.

00:02:46.916 --> 00:02:49.210
New technologies like the
battery, the steam engine,

00:02:49.294 --> 00:02:51.004
and later, electricity,

00:02:51.087 --> 00:02:53.339
opened a world of possibilities,

00:02:53.423 --> 00:02:55.133
including artificial life.

00:02:55.216 --> 00:02:56.467
While the emergent technologies

00:02:56.551 --> 00:02:58.720
became increasingly
linked to imagination,

00:02:58.803 --> 00:03:00.513
magic, and utopia,

00:03:00.597 --> 00:03:02.724
they were also met with
fear and trepidation.

00:03:02.807 --> 00:03:04.726
It wasn't until the 19th century

00:03:04.809 --> 00:03:08.104
that the first human-machine
hybrids appear in fiction.

00:03:08.187 --> 00:03:10.064
Technically,
German Gothic author

00:03:10.148 --> 00:03:12.942
E.T.A. Hoffmann's
1814 "Automata"

00:03:13.026 --> 00:03:14.819
has the first
quasi-human automation,

00:03:14.903 --> 00:03:17.989
but everyone knows
it's a machine.

00:03:18.072 --> 00:03:20.325
In his 1816 story "The Sandman,"

00:03:20.408 --> 00:03:21.826
two men attempt to pass off

00:03:21.910 --> 00:03:25.413
a wooden clockwork-driven
automaton as a real woman

00:03:25.496 --> 00:03:27.457
with moderate success.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:31.085
Jane Loudon's futuristic
1827 novel "The Mummy"

00:03:31.169 --> 00:03:32.921
includes speculative
technologies

00:03:33.004 --> 00:03:34.380
like a mirror holoscreen,

00:03:34.464 --> 00:03:35.798
flying robotic horses,

00:03:35.882 --> 00:03:38.635
and labor androids in the
form of prison guards,

00:03:38.718 --> 00:03:40.303
judges, and lawyers.

00:03:40.386 --> 00:03:41.804
In these texts before

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:44.891
and after their non-human
identities are revealed,

00:03:44.974 --> 00:03:48.686
these creations are viewed with
a mix of revulsion and awe.

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:50.980
Seen as unnatural
and frightening,

00:03:51.064 --> 00:03:52.857
these Romantic era androids

00:03:52.941 --> 00:03:54.859
reflected
the cultural conceptions

00:03:54.943 --> 00:03:56.653
of the human-machine dynamic.

00:03:56.736 --> 00:03:58.571
Edgar Allen Poe
famously explored

00:03:58.655 --> 00:04:00.657
the boundaries of
the mechanical body

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:03.284
and the human body in
his science fiction.

00:04:03.368 --> 00:04:04.744
During his writing career,

00:04:04.827 --> 00:04:07.956
the United States saw over
3,000 miles of train tracks

00:04:08.039 --> 00:04:09.582
laid across the US,

00:04:09.666 --> 00:04:11.167
with both the medical doctor

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:15.129
and machine doctor or mechanic
emerging as professions.

00:04:15.213 --> 00:04:18.591
All of this likely inspired
his 1839 short story

00:04:18.675 --> 00:04:20.510
"The Man That Was Used Up,"

00:04:20.593 --> 00:04:24.639
which is widely cited as the
first true cyborg narrative.

00:04:24.722 --> 00:04:26.933
The tale is a satirical
social mystery of sorts.

00:04:27.016 --> 00:04:28.935
A narrator tries to
determine the story

00:04:29.018 --> 00:04:32.480
behind General
John A.B.C. Smith's

00:04:32.563 --> 00:04:34.816
great notoriety as a war hero.

00:04:34.899 --> 00:04:36.526
Formidable and handsome,

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:38.403
the narrator is
horrified to learn

00:04:38.486 --> 00:04:41.698
that the general he admires
so much lost his teeth,

00:04:41.781 --> 00:04:44.784
arms, legs, scalp,
shoulder, chest, and eye,

00:04:44.867 --> 00:04:47.787
and most of his tongue from
torture while a war prisoner,

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:51.165
and now uses a false
eye, wig, cork legs,

00:04:51.249 --> 00:04:52.625
and other man-made parts,

00:04:52.709 --> 00:04:55.336
including a mechanical tongue.

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:58.715
When the narrator
sees him without his
non-organic additions

00:04:58.798 --> 00:05:02.677
as a large and exceedingly odd
looking bundle of something,

00:05:02.760 --> 00:05:05.596
he's frightened into
shocked silence.

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:07.974
So we've had humans with
machine enhancements

00:05:08.057 --> 00:05:10.560
in fiction for a
couple hundred years,

00:05:10.643 --> 00:05:13.938
but it took until 1960 for
someone to define them.

00:05:14.022 --> 00:05:17.316
The word cyborg
first appears in 1960

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:18.901
in a New York Times
article about scientists

00:05:18.985 --> 00:05:21.779
looking into
enhancing astronauts

00:05:21.863 --> 00:05:23.573
for space travel efficiency.

00:05:23.656 --> 00:05:25.658
Coined by researchers
Manfred E. Clynes

00:05:25.742 --> 00:05:27.618
and Dr. Nathan S. Kline,

00:05:27.702 --> 00:05:29.662
the article proposes development

00:05:29.746 --> 00:05:31.039
of human-and-then-some bodies

00:05:31.122 --> 00:05:33.750
that would not need
to eat or breathe

00:05:33.833 --> 00:05:37.086
because of battery powered
devices built into the body.

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:40.673
They provide us with the first
public definition of cyborg,

00:05:40.757 --> 00:05:42.842
essentially a man-machine system

00:05:42.925 --> 00:05:45.803
in which the control
mechanisms of the human portion

00:05:45.887 --> 00:05:49.682
are modified externally by
drugs or regulatory devices

00:05:49.766 --> 00:05:51.809
so that the being can
live in an environment

00:05:51.893 --> 00:05:53.311
different from the normal one.

00:05:53.394 --> 00:05:56.522
The late 1970s and '80s
were a time of cultural

00:05:56.606 --> 00:05:59.067
and social technological
pandemonium.

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:01.277
The introduction of
personal computers,

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:03.071
portable phones, fiber optics,

00:06:03.154 --> 00:06:06.407
and of course, the internet
challenged our worldview

00:06:06.491 --> 00:06:08.534
and created new anxieties.

00:06:08.618 --> 00:06:10.078
In a post-industrial world,

00:06:10.161 --> 00:06:13.372
existing fears of machines
taking over humans' jobs

00:06:13.456 --> 00:06:16.250
and relevance seemed
even more possible.

00:06:16.334 --> 00:06:17.460
And like always,

00:06:17.543 --> 00:06:19.837
popular culture
exposes that anxiety

00:06:19.921 --> 00:06:22.715
and imagines the possible
effects of digital technology,

00:06:22.799 --> 00:06:24.509
positive and negative,

00:06:24.592 --> 00:06:27.261
in dystopian android movies
like "The Terminator"

00:06:27.345 --> 00:06:28.513
and "Blade Runner,"

00:06:28.596 --> 00:06:30.556
but also more
optimistic characters

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:33.684
like the superhero Cyborg
in DC Comic Universe

00:06:33.768 --> 00:06:36.062
and "Ghost in the Shell's"
crime-fighting cyberpunk

00:06:36.145 --> 00:06:38.022
Major Motoko Kusanagi.

00:06:38.106 --> 00:06:41.484
In 1985, Donna Haraway's
feminist post-human text,

00:06:41.567 --> 00:06:42.985
"A Cyborg Manifesto,"

00:06:43.069 --> 00:06:46.489
declared we are all
already cyborgs.

00:06:46.572 --> 00:06:48.407
She argues that
humans are chimeras

00:06:48.491 --> 00:06:50.201
of organism and machine,

00:06:50.284 --> 00:06:52.370
a reality
we sit with uncomfortably,

00:06:52.453 --> 00:06:54.330
although she argues
we should take pleasure

00:06:54.413 --> 00:06:56.916
in such instability
and transgression.

00:06:56.999 --> 00:06:59.127
Haraway uses the
cyborg as a metaphor

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:01.879
to challenge the definition
of human and non-human.

00:07:01.963 --> 00:07:05.258
She points out that
we are already cyborgs.

00:07:05.341 --> 00:07:07.552
Modern medicine has
advanced to the point

00:07:07.635 --> 00:07:09.887
that small machines
can reside in the body

00:07:09.971 --> 00:07:12.557
in what she refers
to as couplings.

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:15.476
The anxiety around cyborgs
is their perceived threat

00:07:15.560 --> 00:07:18.312
to humanity in
the physical and mental,

00:07:18.396 --> 00:07:22.233
enhanced humans who challenge
the very idea of human.

00:07:22.316 --> 00:07:24.735
Post-humanism is
a philosophical concept

00:07:24.819 --> 00:07:26.821
that challenges
anthropocentrism,

00:07:26.904 --> 00:07:27.905
the idea that humans
are the central

00:07:27.989 --> 00:07:31.409
and most significant
entities in the world.

00:07:31.492 --> 00:07:34.662
It's all about blurring or
eliminating the boundaries

00:07:34.745 --> 00:07:37.790
between humans and other
organic and inorganic matter.

00:07:37.874 --> 00:07:40.626
Monsters and the
post-human are intertwined.

00:07:40.710 --> 00:07:44.255
As N. Katherine Hayles argues
in "How We Became Post-Human,"

00:07:44.338 --> 00:07:46.924
even the threat of
transformation via technology

00:07:47.008 --> 00:07:48.885
inspires simultaneous
terror and pleasure.

00:07:48.968 --> 00:07:50.761
But that does not
mean that post-human

00:07:50.845 --> 00:07:53.890
has to be apocalyptic
or anti-human,

00:07:53.973 --> 00:07:57.018
but you can probably
see how the cyborg

00:07:57.101 --> 00:07:59.353
can easily represent
both the negative

00:07:59.437 --> 00:08:02.231
and positive interpretations
of post-humanism.

00:08:02.315 --> 00:08:04.442
Discoveries in zoology
also contribute

00:08:04.525 --> 00:08:06.652
to our modern
conception of cyborgs.

00:08:06.736 --> 00:08:08.321
By the end of the 20th century,

00:08:08.404 --> 00:08:10.531
the distinction between
the human animal

00:08:10.615 --> 00:08:13.159
and the non-human
animal is narrowed.

00:08:13.242 --> 00:08:16.037
Many non-human animals
craft and use tools,

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:17.955
problem solve,
have complex languages,

00:08:18.039 --> 00:08:21.834
social behaviors,
and grieving practices.

00:08:21.918 --> 00:08:24.378
The cyborg then is
a man-made distinction

00:08:24.462 --> 00:08:26.547
between human
and non-human animal

00:08:26.631 --> 00:08:28.049
where artificial devices

00:08:28.132 --> 00:08:31.052
contribute to making
humans less animal.

00:08:31.135 --> 00:08:32.720
Although a more radical approach

00:08:32.803 --> 00:08:35.348
is to see the cyborg as a
break in the distinction

00:08:35.431 --> 00:08:36.891
between human,
animal, and machine,

00:08:36.974 --> 00:08:39.644
in a post-humanist
utopian vision

00:08:39.727 --> 00:08:42.188
of connectivity among all things

00:08:42.271 --> 00:08:44.106
where boundaries
are not only blurred

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:46.651
but they also
simply don't exist,

00:08:46.734 --> 00:08:49.237
which is both liberating
and terrifying.

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:52.490
This is why the cyborg
is always made monstrous.

00:08:52.573 --> 00:08:55.451
From the hive-mind driven
villainous Borg in Star Trek

00:08:55.534 --> 00:08:57.036
to Marvel's Dr. Octopus

00:08:57.119 --> 00:08:59.956
or James Bond's
nefarious Dr. No,

00:09:00.039 --> 00:09:03.626
evil or antagonistic cyborgs
are physically threatening

00:09:03.709 --> 00:09:05.127
with their divergent
technologies

00:09:05.211 --> 00:09:08.464
marking them as the other
or the bad guy in the story.

00:09:08.548 --> 00:09:10.466
Even when cyborgs
are the good guys,

00:09:10.549 --> 00:09:11.550
this holds true,

00:09:11.634 --> 00:09:13.970
but they are painted
in a different light.

00:09:14.053 --> 00:09:17.306
Robocop, Ironman,
Major Motoko, the Bionic Woman,

00:09:17.390 --> 00:09:18.516
and others like them

00:09:18.599 --> 00:09:20.977
almost always have some
traumatic backstory

00:09:21.060 --> 00:09:24.438
or event that propels them
towards mechanical augmentation,

00:09:24.522 --> 00:09:26.649
justifying their monstrosity.

00:09:26.732 --> 00:09:29.360
Star Wars is the
perfect example.

00:09:29.443 --> 00:09:32.572
Both Darth Vader and Luke
Skywalker are cyborgs,

00:09:32.655 --> 00:09:34.490
at least since the
end of Episode Five.

00:09:34.573 --> 00:09:37.034
We spend "A New Hope" and
"The Empire Strikes Back"

00:09:37.118 --> 00:09:39.620
following Luke's
struggles and triumphs.

00:09:39.704 --> 00:09:41.038
His tragic backstory,

00:09:41.122 --> 00:09:42.999
an orphan relegated
to a hard life

00:09:43.082 --> 00:09:45.084
on an unforgiving planet
after his father was murdered,

00:09:45.167 --> 00:09:48.004
at least that's what
Obiwan tells him,

00:09:48.087 --> 00:09:51.632
creates sympathy even
before his hand is cut off.

00:09:51.716 --> 00:09:55.136
Luke's artificial hand even
looks organic at first.

00:09:55.219 --> 00:09:57.179
Essentially, his cyborg
nature can be hidden.

00:09:57.263 --> 00:10:00.725
Darth Vader's tech,
on the other hand,

00:10:00.808 --> 00:10:02.935
is not only on full display,

00:10:03.019 --> 00:10:05.271
it visually makes
him the villain.

00:10:05.354 --> 00:10:07.815
His cyborgness isn't justified.

00:10:07.898 --> 00:10:10.985
That is, not until
Anakin's own tragic history

00:10:11.068 --> 00:10:14.447
which helped push him to choose
the dark side is revealed.

00:10:14.530 --> 00:10:17.491
I must point out that this
only happens explicitly

00:10:17.575 --> 00:10:18.993
in the newer movies

00:10:19.076 --> 00:10:21.954
released at the turn of
the 21st century when arguably,

00:10:22.038 --> 00:10:24.999
attitudes towards robotics
have somewhat shifted.

00:10:25.082 --> 00:10:28.419
Nevertheless, all cyborgs
still represent the fear

00:10:28.502 --> 00:10:30.921
inherent in losing ourselves
in the technologies we create.

00:10:31.005 --> 00:10:33.924
Cyborgs may not
inherently be monsters,

00:10:34.008 --> 00:10:38.012
but we are afraid they
will become monsters.

00:10:38.095 --> 00:10:40.639
Our response to them is
largely driven, I think,

00:10:40.723 --> 00:10:44.060
by how we want to integrate
technology into our life.

00:10:44.143 --> 00:10:48.564
Do we live with technology
or do we become technology?

00:10:49.857 --> 00:10:51.359
"Liezi." Did I do it right?

00:10:51.442 --> 00:10:52.943
Sliezi includes mention--

00:10:53.027 --> 00:10:54.070
(crew)
Hold on.

00:10:54.153 --> 00:10:55.946
Clockwork-driven ato,

00:10:57.406 --> 00:10:59.575
the automata, automaton.

